1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,215
A ball rolling downhill gives away energy.

2
00:00:03,215 --> 00:00:05,221
A dry sponge pulls in water.

3
00:00:05,221 --> 00:00:07,895
A full glass cannot take one more drop.

4
00:00:08,295 --> 00:00:10,267
Some things welcome one more.

5
00:00:10,267 --> 00:00:11,430
Some refuse it.

6
00:00:11,880 --> 00:00:15,021
Diya sprinkles salt on
her dinner in the kitchen.

7
00:00:15,021 --> 00:00:16,660
Salt is sodium chloride.

8
00:00:17,010 --> 00:00:20,951
To make it, each chlorine atom
grabbed one electron from sodium.

9
00:00:21,451 --> 00:00:25,525
By the end of this video, you will
know which atoms release energy when

10
00:00:25,525 --> 00:00:28,290
they grab an electron,
and which ones refuse.

11
00:00:28,940 --> 00:00:31,895
So what happens when an
atom gains an electron?

12
00:00:32,245 --> 00:00:37,171
We add one electron to an isolated
gas atom, and it becomes a negative

13
00:00:37,171 --> 00:00:38,071
ion.

14
00:00:38,080 --> 00:00:42,351
The enthalpy change in this step
is the electron gain enthalpy.

15
00:00:42,351 --> 00:00:46,463
Enthalpy change means the heat
energy taken in or given out.

16
00:00:46,813 --> 00:00:49,768
If energy is released,
the value is negative.

17
00:00:49,768 --> 00:00:53,021
If energy must be supplied,
the value is positive.

18
00:00:53,621 --> 00:00:56,000
Let's watch it on our molecule bench.

19
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:57,685
Here is a chlorine atom.

20
00:00:57,685 --> 00:00:59,158
Two, eight, seven.

21
00:00:59,508 --> 00:01:02,002
Its outer shell has one empty place.

22
00:01:02,002 --> 00:01:04,596
An electron arrives and drops into it.

23
00:01:04,996 --> 00:01:06,365
Energy comes out.

24
00:01:06,365 --> 00:01:08,290
The bar falls below zero.

25
00:01:08,690 --> 00:01:11,825
Minus three hundred forty
nine kilojoules per mole.

26
00:01:11,825 --> 00:01:15,248
Chlorine becomes a chloride
ion, two, eight, eight.

27
00:01:15,648 --> 00:01:16,874
Now try helium.

28
00:01:16,874 --> 00:01:19,119
Its only shell is already full.

29
00:01:19,519 --> 00:01:23,878
The new electron must go into a brand
new shell, far from the nucleus.

30
00:01:24,278 --> 00:01:26,324
So energy must be pushed in.

31
00:01:26,324 --> 00:01:28,156
The bar rises above zero.

32
00:01:28,156 --> 00:01:30,659
Plus forty eight kilojoules per mole.

33
00:01:31,309 --> 00:01:33,548
Now the halogens, group seventeen.

34
00:01:33,548 --> 00:01:35,835
Fluorine, minus three twenty eight.

35
00:01:35,835 --> 00:01:38,027
Chlorine, minus three forty nine.

36
00:01:38,427 --> 00:01:40,805
Bromine, minus three twenty five.

37
00:01:40,805 --> 00:01:43,029
Iodine, minus two ninety five.

38
00:01:43,429 --> 00:01:46,040
And the noble gases are all positive.

39
00:01:46,040 --> 00:01:48,319
Neon, plus one hundred sixteen.

40
00:01:48,319 --> 00:01:50,122
Argon, plus ninety six.

41
00:01:50,772 --> 00:01:53,634
Why do halogens release the most energy?

42
00:01:53,634 --> 00:01:54,792
Step by step.

43
00:01:55,142 --> 00:01:56,042
Step one.

44
00:01:55,996 --> 00:01:59,371
A halogen has seven
electrons in its outer shell.

45
00:02:00,121 --> 00:02:01,021
Step two.

46
00:02:00,985 --> 00:02:05,006
One more electron gives it
the full shell of a noble gas.

47
00:02:05,756 --> 00:02:06,675
Step three.

48
00:02:06,675 --> 00:02:11,286
The nucleus pulls the incoming electron
strongly, because the atom is small

49
00:02:11,286 --> 00:02:12,315
for its row.

50
00:02:13,065 --> 00:02:14,006
Step four.

51
00:02:14,006 --> 00:02:16,960
So a large amount of energy is released.

52
00:02:16,960 --> 00:02:18,735
A large negative value.

53
00:02:19,485 --> 00:02:22,316
But look at fluorine and
chlorine side by side.

54
00:02:22,316 --> 00:02:25,931
Fluorine is smaller, so you
would expect it to release more.

55
00:02:26,331 --> 00:02:29,004
Fluorine's second shell
is tiny and crowded.

56
00:02:29,004 --> 00:02:32,559
The new electron is pushed by
the electrons already there.

57
00:02:32,959 --> 00:02:37,297
Chlorine's third shell is roomier,
so the new electron feels less push.

58
00:02:37,697 --> 00:02:38,904
Pause and think.

59
00:02:38,904 --> 00:02:43,635
Which releases more energy when it
gains an electron, fluorine or chlorine?

60
00:02:46,835 --> 00:02:47,735
Chlorine!

61
00:02:47,608 --> 00:02:52,157
Minus three forty nine is more
negative than minus three twenty eight.

62
00:02:52,157 --> 00:02:54,924
Oxygen and sulphur show the same pattern.

63
00:02:55,574 --> 00:02:56,736
Remember this.

64
00:02:56,736 --> 00:03:01,243
More energy released means a more
negative electron gain enthalpy.

65
00:03:01,943 --> 00:03:03,678
Now, one worked example.

66
00:03:03,978 --> 00:03:08,505
Chlorine's electron gain enthalpy
is minus three forty nine kilojoules

67
00:03:08,505 --> 00:03:09,411
per mole.

68
00:03:09,411 --> 00:03:14,247
How much energy is released when
three point five five grams of gaseous

69
00:03:14,247 --> 00:03:16,963
chlorine atoms each gain an electron?

70
00:03:17,463 --> 00:03:21,857
The molar mass of chlorine is thirty
five point five grams per mole.

71
00:03:22,607 --> 00:03:23,924
Moles of atoms.

72
00:03:23,924 --> 00:03:28,992
Three point five five divided by thirty
five point five is zero point one

73
00:03:28,992 --> 00:03:29,892
mole.

74
00:03:30,367 --> 00:03:34,069
Energy released is zero point
one times three forty nine.

75
00:03:34,069 --> 00:03:36,841
That is thirty four point nine kilojoules.

76
00:03:37,591 --> 00:03:40,263
Released energy carries a minus sign.

77
00:03:40,263 --> 00:03:44,837
So the enthalpy change is minus
thirty four point nine kilojoules.

78
00:03:45,487 --> 00:03:46,953
Now, the exam trap.

79
00:03:47,303 --> 00:03:51,559
Some students see minus three twenty
eight and minus three forty nine,

80
00:03:51,559 --> 00:03:55,455
and say fluorine's value is
bigger, so fluorine releases more.

81
00:03:55,855 --> 00:03:59,096
But here, more negative
means more energy released.

82
00:03:59,096 --> 00:04:03,671
Chlorine, minus three forty nine,
releases the most of all the elements.

83
00:04:04,321 --> 00:04:05,887
Where is this asked?

84
00:04:05,887 --> 00:04:10,542
JEE Main and NEET both ask you to
order electron gain enthalpies, with

85
00:04:10,542 --> 00:04:13,012
the fluorine and chlorine exception.

86
00:04:13,612 --> 00:04:14,893
Exam level now.

87
00:04:14,893 --> 00:04:19,899
Arrange fluorine, chlorine, bromine
and iodine by the energy released when

88
00:04:19,899 --> 00:04:21,680
each gains an electron.

89
00:04:21,680 --> 00:04:23,015
Pause and solve.

90
00:04:28,415 --> 00:04:29,695
The answer is B.

91
00:04:29,695 --> 00:04:33,187
Chlorine, then fluorine,
then bromine, then iodine.

92
00:04:33,687 --> 00:04:37,660
Down the group, the value gets
less negative, as the atoms grow.

93
00:04:38,160 --> 00:04:41,844
But fluorine's crowded small
shell lowers it below chlorine.

94
00:04:42,344 --> 00:04:44,322
It still stays above bromine.

95
00:04:44,322 --> 00:04:47,111
Three twenty eight
beats three twenty five.

96
00:04:47,111 --> 00:04:48,990
C dropped fluorine too far.

97
00:04:49,590 --> 00:04:51,050
Here is your recap.

98
00:04:51,050 --> 00:04:52,550
Screenshot this card.

99
00:04:55,750 --> 00:04:58,587
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

100
00:04:58,987 --> 00:04:59,887
One.

101
00:04:59,509 --> 00:05:03,027
What does a negative electron
gain enthalpy tell you?

102
00:05:03,827 --> 00:05:04,727
Two.

103
00:05:04,377 --> 00:05:07,540
Why are the values for
noble gases positive?

104
00:05:08,340 --> 00:05:09,240
Three.

105
00:05:08,969 --> 00:05:12,404
Why does chlorine release
more energy than fluorine?

106
00:05:13,304 --> 00:05:18,075
Next time: when two atoms share
electrons, which one pulls them closer?

107
00:05:18,075 --> 00:05:20,299
We find out in electronegativity.
